---
id: 1c63d361-8065-4f8d-bdab-8f6e5325141c
title: Leucopenia
---

# Leucopenia

- **Definition** - reduction of total number of circulating white cells, caused by a reduction in all types of white cells, or an individual cell type (can occur in isolation, or as part of reduction in all 3 haematological lineages, i.e. pancytopenia) 
![](../img/interpretation_of_wbc_differentials.png)
- **Neutropenia:**
  - **Definition** - reduction of absolute neutrophil count (ANC) \< 1.5 x 10<sup>9</sup>/L, classified by severity:
    - <u>Neutropenia</u> - ANC \< 1.5 x 10<sup>9</sup>/L
    - <u>Severe neutropenia</u> - ANC \< 0.5 x 10<sup>9</sup>/L, and is considered threshold for risk of severe bacterial infections
    - <u>Profound neutropenia</u> - ANC \< 0.1 x 10<sup>9</sup>/L
  - **Etiology of neutropenia:**
    - **Drug-induced neutropenia** (not uncommon):
      - <u>Antithyroid drugs</u> - carbimazole, propylthiouracil
      - <u>Anti-arrhythmics</u> - quinidine, procainamide
      - <u>Analgesics/ anti-inflammatory agents</u> - gold, penicillamine, naproxen
      - <u>Anti-hypertensives</u> - captopril, enalapril, nifedipine
      - <u>Anti-convulsants</u> - phenytoin, sodium valproate, carbamazepine
      - <u>Antibiotics</u> - sulphonamides, beta-lactams
      - <u>Antidepressants</u> - amitriptyline, dosulepin, minanserin
      - <u>Antimalarial</u> - pyrimethamine, dapsone, sulfadoxine, chloroquine

      
      ![](../img/drug_induced_neutropenia.png)
    - **Haematological disease resulting in bone marrow infiltration** - leukaemia, myelodysplasia
    - **Autoimmune** - connective tissue disease (immune-mediated neutropenia)
    - **Miscellaneous** - viral, salmonella, malaria, Kostmann's syndrome, Afro-Caribbean and Middle Eastern descent
  - **Clinical features of neutropenia:**
    - <u>Asymptomatic</u> - absence of if risk of infection is low due to short period and mild neutropenia
    - <u>Neutropenic fever</u> - fever is the first and often only manifestation of infection
    - <u>Sepsis</u> - ocassionally patient may present w/ overwhelming sepsis, as the lack of neutrophils as first-line of defense allows patients to become septicaemic and shocked within hours of bacteraemia w/o immediate ABx therapy
- **Lymphopenia:**
  - **Definition** - reduction of absolute lymphocyte count \< 1 x 10<sup>9</sup>/L
  - **Etiology of lymphopenia:**
    - <u>Drug-induced lymphopenia</u> - e.g. corticosteroids, cytotoxic agents
    - <u>HIV related</u> - chronic HIV infection
    - <u>Congenital immunodeficiency</u> - severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID)
    - <u>Chronic inflammatory disease</u> - connective tissue disease
    - <u>Miscellaneous</u> - renal failure, sarcoidosis, lymphoma
  - **Clinical features of lymphopenia:**
    - <u>Asymptomatic</u> - minor reductions may be asymptomatic
    - <u>Risk of intracellular infections</u> - e.g. fungal, viral and mycobacterial infections
    - <u>Secondary malignancies</u> - risk of lymphoproliferative disease, and solid organ tumours (esp those associated w/ viral infections such as EBV, HPV and HHV-8)
